CAT = "ukcareComms"; SEG = "COM"
# Standard 6 Communication, Standard 14 Handling information
Q = [
("Which of these is non-verbal communication?",
 "Facial expression", ["Speaking slowly", "A phone call", "A handwritten letter"],
 "Non-verbal communication includes facial expressions, gestures, body language, eye contact and touch."),
("What is active listening?",
 "Focusing fully and checking you understood", ["Talking while the person is still speaking", "Writing notes without any eye contact", "Planning your reply while they talk"],
 "Active listening means focusing on the speaker, showing interest, letting them finish and checking back what they meant."),
("A person who wears a hearing aid seems not to follow you. What should you check first?",
 "That the aid is in and working", ["That the TV is turned up loudly", "That you are shouting clearly", "That a relative is present"],
 "Check the hearing aid is fitted, switched on, clean and has a working battery. Then face the person, reduce background noise and speak clearly."),
("How should you speak to a person with hearing loss who lip-reads?",
 "Face them and speak clearly", ["Shout clearly from across the room", "Cover your mouth to be polite", "Speak very slowly and loudly"],
 "Face the person with light on your face, speak clearly at a normal pace and do not exaggerate lip movements or shout."),
("What is Makaton?",
 "Signs and symbols used with speech", ["A type of digital hearing aid", "A braille system for reading", "A sign language used in Wales"],
 "Makaton uses signs and symbols alongside speech to support people who have difficulty communicating, such as some people with a learning disability."),
("What does it mean that behaviour can be a form of communication?",
 "Actions can show needs or feelings", ["Behaviour should always be ignored", "Behaviour only shows bad manners", "Behaviour is never linked to needs"],
 "A change in behaviour, such as agitation or withdrawal, may be how a person shows pain, fear, boredom or another need they cannot put into words."),
("Which standard requires health and adult social care services in England to meet the communication needs of disabled people?",
 "The Accessible Information Standard", ["The Caldicott principles", "The Mental Capacity Act 2005", "The Communication Access Code"],
 "The Accessible Information Standard requires NHS and publicly funded adult social care services to identify, record, flag, share and meet people's information and communication needs."),
("What is an example of assistive technology for communication?",
 "A talking tablet", ["A raised toilet seat", "A wheeled walking frame", "A pressure cushion"],
 "Communication aids range from picture boards to tablets and devices that speak typed or chosen words."),
("A person speaks little English. What is the best way to discuss their care?",
 "Use a trained interpreter", ["Ask their young grandchild", "Use louder simple English", "Ask a passing visitor to help"],
 "A trained interpreter gives accurate, confidential communication. Using children or untrained people risks errors and breaches privacy."),
("Which of these is a barrier to communication?",
 "Background noise", ["Good lighting", "Sitting at eye level", "Using the person's name"],
 "Noise, poor lighting, jargon, distractions and unfamiliar language are common barriers. Reduce them before an important conversation."),
("Which question is open?",
 "How are you feeling today?", ["Are you feeling well today?", "Would you like some tea?", "Is that bath too hot?"],
 "Open questions invite fuller answers. Closed questions, answered yes or no, are useful for people who find long replies hard."),
("What is meant by confidentiality in care?",
 "Sharing information only when justified", ["Never sharing anything with anyone", "Sharing only with the person's friends", "Telling families everything they ask"],
 "Confidential information is shared only with those who need it to give care, with consent where possible, or when there is a legal or safety reason."),
("When may you share confidential information without the person's consent?",
 "When someone is at risk of harm", ["When a relative phones to ask", "When a colleague is curious", "When the person is asleep"],
 "Confidentiality can be overridden to protect someone from serious harm, such as in a safeguarding concern. Follow agreed ways of working and tell your manager."),
("Which laws govern how personal data is handled in the UK?",
 "UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act", ["The Care Act 2014 and the Children Act", "The Equality Act and Human Rights Act", "The Mental Capacity Act 2005"],
 "UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018 set the rules for collecting, storing and sharing personal data, including health information."),
("How should you correct a mistake in a handwritten care record?",
 "One line through it, initialled", ["Cover it with correction fluid", "Tear out the page and rewrite it", "Scribble over it until it is hidden"],
 "The original entry must stay readable. Draw a single line through it, write the correction, and sign or initial and date it."),
("Which of these makes a care record entry good?",
 "Factual, timed and signed", ["Written at the end of the week", "Full of your opinions", "Written in pencil to edit later"],
 "Records should be accurate, factual, legible, written promptly and dated, timed and signed so others can rely on them."),
("Where should paper care records be kept when not in use?",
 "Locked away securely", ["On the lounge table", "In your car overnight", "In the person's doorway"],
 "Records containing personal information must be stored securely so only authorised people can see them."),
("You think a care record may have been seen by someone who should not have seen it. What should you do?",
 "Report it to your manager", ["Wait to see if anyone complains", "Tell the person it was nothing", "Delete the record to be safe"],
 "A possible data breach must be reported straight away so the organisation can assess it. Breaches that put people's rights at risk must be reported to the Information Commissioner's Office within 72 hours of the organisation becoming aware."),
("Who has the right to ask to see the information held about them?",
 "The person it is about", ["Any visitor to the home", "Any member of staff", "Only the person's own GP"],
 "People have the right to access their own personal data, known as a subject access request. Others need a lawful reason and proper authorisation."),
("What is good practice for a password to an electronic care record?",
 "Keep it private to you", ["Write it on the screen edge", "Share it with the shift team", "Use the same one as a colleague"],
 "Passwords protect personal data. Keep yours private, log out when you finish and report anyone using your login."),
("A caller says they are a person's daughter and asks about their health. What should you do?",
 "Follow procedure first", ["Tell them everything they ask about", "Hang up the phone without speaking", "Give them a brief update anyway"],
 "You cannot confirm a caller's identity by phone alone. Follow agreed ways of working, check the person's consent and who is authorised before sharing anything."),
("Why is it important to record care accurately?",
 "So others can give safe care", ["So you look busy on each shift", "So any mistakes can be hidden", "So the record looks complete"],
 "Accurate records tell the next worker what happened and what is needed, and give legal evidence of care."),
("Which of these is a secure way to handle information on a phone used for work?",
 "Use only the approved work app", ["Text details on your own phone", "Post updates in a group chat", "Photograph records for later"],
 "Use only systems your employer has approved. Personal phones and messaging groups risk data being lost or seen by the wrong people."),
("A person has limited speech. How could you check what they want for lunch?",
 "Show the options or pictures", ["Choose the most popular meal", "Ask a colleague to decide", "Give the same as yesterday"],
 "Showing real food or picture cards lets the person point to their choice, supporting their communication and independence."),
]
